### CSS variables

OKLCH is the canonical form — declare colours in OKLCH for perceptual uniformity across displays. The hex values in the comments are derivation references, not the runtime values.

```css
:root {
  --bg:           oklch(... ... ...);     /* #...... */
  --surface:      oklch(... ... ...);     /* #...... */
  --ink:          oklch(... ... ...);     /* #...... */
  --mute:         oklch(... ... ...);     /* #...... */
  --border:       oklch(... ... ...);     /* #...... */
  --accent:       oklch(... ... ...);     /* #...... */
  --accent-fg:    oklch(... ... ...);     /* #...... */
  --success:      oklch(... ... ...);     /* #...... */
  --destructive:  oklch(... ... ...);     /* #...... */
  --warn-bg:      oklch(... ... ...);     /* #...... */
}
```

**Why OKLCH, not hex.** Perceptually uniform; preserves saturation on wide-gamut displays where hex is sRGB-clamped.

### Contrast (WCAG-AA verified)

| Pair                            | Ratio    | Status      |
|---------------------------------|----------|-------------|
| `--ink` on `--bg`               | ~XX:1    | AA+ body    |
| `--mute` on `--bg`              | ~X.X:1   | AA body     |
| ...                             |          |             |

### Usage rules

- **Accent ≤ 10% surface area.**
- **No `#000`, no `#fff`.**
- **No pure greys.** Every neutral carries low chroma toward the brand hue.
- **Allergen / regulatory badges**: `--destructive` text + border on `--warn-bg`. Icon + text always.

